                "text": "**Analyzing the Examples**\n\nOkay, I'm digging into these examples now, focusing on the input dimensions and how they relate to the output coordinates. Specifically, I'm trying to see if there's a clear mapping between the input grid (like 4x4 or 2x6) and the changes made to the coordinates in the output. I am making rapid progress.\n\n\n**Revisiting Grid Expansion**\n\nOkay, I've circled back to Example 1, focusing specifically on how the values expand within the grid. I am seeing that the expansion seems edge-relative. For instance, the number 2 at (1,1) moves toward the edges with a shorter distance. The number 3's behavior is as expected as it moves towards the nearest edge. I need to test this theory on a different example!\n\n\n**Determining Expansion Direction**\n\nOkay, I've got a better handle on the expansion direction now, using those examples. It seems each value expands towards the nearest edge based on column position. So, the distance to the left edge or right edge decides which direction a point will expand. I'm seeing this trend consistently and have adjusted my thinking to account for edge proximity!\n\n\n**Refining Edge Proximity**\n\nI've been examining Example 3 closely, where I initially believed expansion was only column-relative. I've realized expansion occurs towards the *closest* edge, in both dimensions! So a value's coordinates are expanded based on distance to the top/bottom edges and left/right edges. For instance, the number 7 expands up and left because it's closer to the top and left edges, respectively. Similarly, the number 8 expands down and right since it is closer to those edges.\n\n\n**Confirming Coordinate Expansion**\n\nI'm now diving deeper into the edge proximity concept, using several new examples. I'm verifying the initial hypothesis regarding how values expand and the closest edge, in terms of both rows and columns. I'm excited about how this new perspective fits with the observed output! I am confident in the direction of this analysis.\n\n\n",
